        <body>
            <div xml:id="d41">

                <pb facs="0171" n="145" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76v30/pg145"/>

                <head>41. Memorandum From A. Denis
                        Clift of the National Security Council Staff to Secretary of
                    State Kissinger<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d41-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1975-04-03">April 3, 1975</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>US-Greek Base Negotiations</item>
                </list>
                <p>We are now approaching the second round of US-Greek base talks, scheduled to
                    begin in Athens on April 7.</p>
                <p>State, without White House clearance, has sent the message at Tab A<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d41-f2"/> to Embassy Athens, providing guidance for the talks.</p>
                <p>
                    NSSM 215, U.S. Security Policy Toward
                        Greece,<anchor n="3" xml:id="d41-f3"/> was issued to ensure that
                    preparations for the talks would go forward in the NSC system, this bearing in mind such contentious interagency
                    issues as homeporting [<hi rendition="#italic">1 line not
                    declassified</hi>].</p>
                <p>The NSSM response<anchor n="4" xml:id="d41-f4"/>
                    worked a very useful effect in that it brought DOD around and led to interagency agreement [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">2½ lines not declassified</hi>].</p>
                <p>The NSSM response also reached the conclusion
                    that, despite the internal U.S. Navy decision to give up homeporting as soon as
                    possible, this “concession” to the Greeks should be in return for meaningful
                    Greek concessions. This quid pro quo approach has been weakened in the
                    instructions at Tab A; Navy clearly plans to give up homeporting in this round,
                    concessions or not.</p>
                <pb facs="0172" n="146" xml:id="pg146"/>
                <p>The approach the U.S. Government is taking in these talks—[<hi
                        rendition="#italic">less than 1 line not declassified</hi>] to U.S.-Greek
                    and Greek-NATO linkages, to
                    homeporting—involves interagency policy issues that should be confirmed in a
                    decision memorandum. I recommend that action be taken on Log #1737, March 21,
                    1975, which forwarded the NSSM 215 response
                    together with a proposed NSDM.<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d41-f5"/>
                </p>
                <p rendition="#flush">
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Recommendation</hi>
                </p>
                <p>That action be taken on the NSDM forwarded with
                        NSC Log #1737.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d41-f6"
                    />
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
